  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best solution is to <b>Include a Global Secondary Index (GSI) optimized for top score retrieval and implement DAX for in-memory caching</b>.<br><br>This design leverages a GSI to make querying the highest scores extremely efficient—since GSIs allow for fast, flexible queries on non-primary attributes (such as top scores). AWS DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) is a purpose-built in-memory cache for DynamoDB, providing microsecond response times for repeated reads, and is ideal for workloads where cached high-score data is frequently accessed. DAX is fully managed by AWS and integrates seamlessly with DynamoDB APIs.<br><br><i>Structure a DynamoDB table with significantly higher read throughput compared to write throughput, using CloudFront as a caching layer</i> is not optimal because CloudFront is a CDN mainly for caching static web content (HTML, images, etc.) and is not designed for dynamic, database-driven APIs with personalized score data.<br><br><i>Deploy a DynamoDB table balanced for similar read and write throughput, leveraging CloudFront caching</i> does not align with your access pattern—your reads vastly outweigh writes, and again, CloudFront is not suited for low-latency, dynamic API responses.<br><br><i>Use a DynamoDB table provisioned for much higher read capacity, and pair it with ElastiCache for caching</i> may seem logical, but ElastiCache requires you to manage data-invalidation, synchronization, and integration with DynamoDB manually. DAX, in contrast, is a managed and purpose-built caching service for DynamoDB, simplifying both integration and performance. <br><br><b>Tips &amp; Tricks:</b><ul><li>For highly-skewed read workloads, always consider caching.</li><li>DAX is plug-and-play with DynamoDB, offering both performance improvements and operational simplicity over alternatives like ElastiCache.</li><li>Use GSIs for flexible, efficient queries on secondary attributes (such as top scores).</li></ul></p>",
  "query": "<p>You are designing an API to store and retrieve video game scores using DynamoDB.<br>In your workload, reads outnumber writes by a factor of 100. High-frequency access occurs for the top 1% of scores, mainly because users are pulling high scores.<br>What is the most suitable approach for building this solution on DynamoDB?</p>",
